TITLE:: RLScale
summary:: A scale as the pitch system of its degrees
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLEDO, Classes/RLRegions, Classes/RLChordSpace, Classes/RLFit, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
Some pitch classes of a system, in order from a tonic. The scale's pitch classes are its degrees, 0 (the tonic) to n - 1, and its pitches Integers (degree and octave, pitch 0 the tonic at or above the parent's origin). It answers what an link::Classes/RLEDO:: answers, so chord spaces, relations, graphs, walks and voice leading work on degrees as they do on the steps of a division: one step is one degree, whatever its size. Heights are the true ones, through the parent, so a walk on the degrees sounds in the parent's tuning.

A scale is not a morphism of intervals: one step is a tone or a semitone of the parent (link::#-specific::).

A minor scale holds two forms (Schoenberg, emphasis::Structural Functions of Harmony::, chapter II): its degrees are the descending (natural) form, and its sixth and seventh degrees have a substitute, kept in link::#-alternates::.

code::
~c = RLScale.major(RLEDO(12));
~c.degrees;                             // [0, 2, 4, 5, 7, 9, 11]
~c.toParent(8);                         // 14: degree 1 an octave up
~c.specific(2);                         // [4, 3]: a third is major or minor
~tri = ~c.triads;                       // a chord space: one type, [0, 2, 4]
~tri.all.collect { |ch| ~c.numeral(ch) };   // I ii iii IV V vi viio
~c.progression(~tri.chord(4), ~tri.chord(0));   // ascending
RLRelationContext.singleStep(~tri).graph.neighbours(~tri.chord(0));   // iii and vi: the ring of thirds
RLScale.major(RLJust([3, 5])).degrees;  // the just major scale on the lattice
RLScale.minor(RLEDO(12), 9).alternates; // [[5, 6], [6, 8]]: F sharp and G sharp in A minor
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: parent
The pitch system the scale lives in.
argument:: pcs
Pitch classes of the parent, the tonic first, the rest in any order. Two on one pitch class are an Error.
argument:: alternates
code::[[degree, pitch class], ...]::, other forms of some degrees.
argument:: label

METHOD:: major
The tones of the major triads on IV, I and V of a tonic (the parent's zero when none is given), in any system: 12 steps, 19, 22, the just lattice.

METHOD:: minor
The tones of the minor triads on iv, i and v, with the substitutes of the sixth and seventh degrees.

METHOD:: fifths
A chain of fifths, code::below:: the tonic and code::above:: it.

METHOD:: modes
The seven diatonic modes, by how many of a mode's seven fifths lie below its tonic: code::\lydian:: (none), code::\major::, code::\mixolydian::, code::\dorian::, code::\aeolian::, code::\phrygian::, code::\locrian:: (six).

METHOD:: below
That number for a mode's name. code::\ionian:: is code::\major::; code::\minor:: stands where code::\aeolian:: does.

METHOD:: modal
A diatonic mode about a tonic: the tones of a major scale from another of its degrees (the Mixolydian on G is C major from G, in any tuning). code::\major:: and code::\minor:: are the two scales above, the minor with its two substitutes; the other modes have their seven tones and no substitute.
code::
RLScale.modal(RLEDO(12), 7, \mixolydian).degrees;   // [7, 9, 11, 0, 2, 4, 5]
RLScale.modal(RLEDO(12), 2, \dorian).degrees;       // [2, 4, 5, 7, 9, 11, 0]
RLScale.modal(RLJust([3, 5]), nil, \dorian).degrees; // on the lattice: the just major scale from its second degree
::
argument:: parent
argument:: tonic
argument:: name
argument:: leading
True gives the Mixolydian, the Dorian and the Aeolian the tone a semitone below the tonic as a substitute (the leading tone of their cadences), as the minor scale has its raised seventh. A mode with it holds eight tones, and so the music of more keys than its own: a reading that offered such modes everywhere took the Mixolydian on G for A minor.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: parent
METHOD:: degrees
The parent's pitch classes, tonic first, ascending.
METHOD:: alternates
METHOD:: tonic
METHOD:: tones
Every pitch class the scale holds, the natural ones first.

METHOD:: parentPc
The parent's pitch class of a degree (its natural form).
METHOD:: degreeOf
The degree of a parent's pitch class, natural or alternate; nil outside the scale.
METHOD:: holds
METHOD:: isNatural

METHOD:: toParent
The parent's pitch of a pitch of the scale.
METHOD:: fromParent
And back; nil for a pitch outside the natural form.

METHOD:: specific
The sizes a number of steps takes in the parent: two for each in a diatonic scale.
METHOD:: generic
The numbers of steps that span a parent's interval somewhere in the scale.

METHOD:: interval
A just ratio as a number of steps.
METHOD:: pitchInterval

METHOD:: triads
METHOD:: sevenths
METHOD:: chords
Chord spaces on the scale: a root and every second degree above it.

METHOD:: realize
A chord of the scale as pitch classes of the parent.
METHOD:: in
As a chord of a space of the parent; nil when the space has none with these tones.
METHOD:: numeral
The Roman numeral of a chord of thirds: capitals for a major third, o and + for a smaller and a larger fifth.

METHOD:: progression
Schoenberg's kind of root progression between two chords: code::\ascending:: (strong: a fourth up, a third down), code::\descending:: (a fourth down, a third up), code::\superstrong:: (a step), code::\same::.
METHOD:: progressionOf
The same from a number of steps.

METHOD:: mode
The same tones from another degree.
METHOD:: commonTones
